Article and FeatureArticle – Java OOP

Project Description

This project demonstrates Inheritance and Method Overriding in Java using a publishing scenario.

The "Article" class contains the basic article details such as "title" and "wordCount". The "FeatureArticle" class extends "Article" and adds an "authorBio" attribute. The "display()" method is overridden to display all the details.

Objective

- To demonstrate inheritance in Java.
- To use constructors for initializing values.
- To implement method overriding.
- To understand the relationship between a parent class and subclass.

OOP Concepts Used

- Inheritance
- Constructor
- Method Overriding
- "super()" keyword

Class Structure

Article
 ├── title
 ├── wordCount
 └── display()
        │
        │ extends
        ↓
FeatureArticle
 ├── authorBio
 └── display()  ← Overridden

Program

class Article {
    String title;
    int wordCount;

    Article(String title, int wordCount) {
        this.title = title;
        this.wordCount = wordCount;
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println("Title: " + title);
        System.out.println("Word Count: " + wordCount);
    }
}

class FeatureArticle extends Article {
    String authorBio;

    FeatureArticle(String title, int wordCount, String authorBio) {
        super(title, wordCount);
        this.authorBio = authorBio;
    }

    @Override
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Feature Article Details");
        System.out.println("Title: " + title);
        System.out.println("Word Count: " + wordCount);
        System.out.println("Author Bio: " + authorBio);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        FeatureArticle article = new FeatureArticle(
            "Artificial Intelligence",
            1200,
            "AI Researcher"
        );

        article.display();
    }
}

Output

Feature Article Details
Title: Artificial Intelligence
Word Count: 1200
Author Bio: AI Researcher

Real-World Application

This concept can be used in news portals, publishing systems, blogs, and content management applications, where different article types share common properties but may have additional information.

Result

The program successfully demonstrates inheritance and method overriding using the "Article" and "FeatureArticle" classes.

Conclusion

This project shows how a subclass can inherit properties and methods from a parent class and override a method to provide its own implementation.
